╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ golomb_sidon_hybrid                                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ The baseline performs a random local search over a small integer set, which is            │
│                          │ inefficient and doesn't exploit the structure of the problem. The key insight is that to  │
│                          │ maximize |A - A| while minimizing |A + A|, we want sets where all pairwise differences    │
│                          │ are distinct (a Golomb ruler) and all pairwise sums are as concentrated as possible.      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ A perfect Golomb ruler has |A - A| = n(n-1)+1 (maximum possible for n elements), giving   │
│                          │ the largest possible numerator. For the denominator, we want sums to collide heavily.     │
│                          │ Sets of the form {0, 1, 2, ..., n-1} have |A + A| = 2n-1 (very small), but their          │
│                          │ difference set is only about n^2/2 (all differences -n+1 to n-1).                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The novel approach: use a "shifted Golomb ruler" combined with a small dense cluster. By  │
│                          │ taking a Golomb ruler G (which maximizes distinct differences) and adding a small         │
│                          │ arithmetic progression offset, we can create sets where differences from the Golomb part  │
│                          │ are all distinct while sums partially collide due to the dense cluster structure.         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Specifically, I'll construct sets of the form A = G ∪ (G + c) for carefully chosen c, or  │
│                          │ use a Sidon-like construction where we take a Golomb ruler and "fold" it. Another         │
│                          │ approach: use sets of the form {a_i} where a_i = i for a small dense core plus large      │
│                          │ Golomb-like gaps.                                                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ My algorithm: generate many candidate sets using:                                         │
│                          │ 1. Random Golomb rulers via greedy/random construction                                    │
│                          │ 2. Sets formed by taking a dense interval [0, k] and adding a Golomb ruler scaled far     │
│                          │ away                                                                                      │
│                          │ 3. Sets where we take {0,1,...,m} ∪ {large Golomb-like values}                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Then score each and keep the best. The dense interval contributes many collisions in the  │
│                          │ sum set (small |A+A|) while the Golomb part contributes many distinct differences. The    │
│                          │ interaction between the two parts creates additional distinct differences.                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I'll implement a hybrid: start with a dense core {0,1,...,m}, then greedily add elements  │
│                          │ that maximize the ratio improvement, using simulated annealing with the actual objective  │
│                          │ function. This directly optimizes the target metric rather than relying on structural     │
│                          │ heuristics alone.                                                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Key improvement over baseline: use a much larger search space, smarter initialization     │
│                          │ (dense core + greedy extensions), and a proper simulated annealing schedule that can      │
│                          │ grow/shrink the set and make large jumps. Also, evaluate the score incrementally where    │
│                          │ possible.                                                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The main construction I'll use: A = {0, 1, ..., m} ∪ B where B is a set of values chosen  │
│                          │ so that B - B and (B - {0..m}) are all distinct, while B + {0..m} overlaps with {0..2m}   │
│                          │ to keep sums small. This exploits the "sum concentration" from the dense core.            │
