╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ sidon_greedy_anneal                                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ The goal is to maximize log|A-A|/log|A+A|. This ratio is maximized when A is close to a   │
│                          │ Sidon set (all pairwise sums distinct, so |A+A| ≈ n(n+1)/2) while the difference set is   │
│                          │ as large as possible (ideally n(n-1) distinct nonzero differences).                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Key insights for improvement over the random baseline:                                    │
│                          │ 1. Structured construction beats random search. A classic strong family is the "greedy    │
│                          │ Sidon" / modular construction: take A = {2k*a + (k^2 mod p)} style sets, or use the       │
│                          │ Singer/Bose-Chowla style constructions. A simpler effective family is A = {i : i in       │
│                          │ [0,n)} ∪ {large*i + i^2} which gives many distinct differences.                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. A very effective practical family: take A = {x_i = i*M + (i*i mod p)} where M is large │
│                          │ enough to separate residues, producing a near-Sidon set with n(n-1) distinct differences  │
│                          │ and only ~n(n+1)/2 sums.                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. I use a Bose-Chowla-like construction: pick a prime p, let n = p-1, and set A = {i : i │
│                          │ mod (p-1)} combined with a quadratic residue offset. Concretely, use A = {i + p*(i*i mod  │
│                          │ p) : i in 0..p-1} — this yields distinct differences because the map i -> (i, i^2) mod p  │
│                          │ has the Sidon property (a+b=c+d implies {a,b}={c,d}).                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 4. Then locally improve: try several primes p, evaluate the actual score, and do a short  │
│                          │ simulated-annealing / hill-climb on the resulting set to squeeze out extra differences.   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 5. To keep |A+A| small while |A-A| large, we want the set to be Sidon (B_2 set) — the     │
│                          │ quadratic construction A = {i + p*i^2} gives a perfect B_2 set modulo p, hence distinct   │
│                          │ sums, while differences are also mostly distinct.                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Implementation: generate candidates from the quadratic construction for many small        │
│                          │ primes, score them, pick the best, then run a short local search (perturb elements,       │
│                          │ accept if score improves). Return the best set found within the time budget.              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The score function is computed efficiently using sets. For n up to ~200 this runs fast.   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Let me implement:                                                                         │
│                          │ - `_score(a)` same as before.                                                             │
│                          │ - `_quadratic_set(p)`: returns  — a Sidon-like set of size p.                             │
│                          │ - Try p in a range, pick best.                                                            │
│                          │ - Then hill-climb with small perturbations.                                               │
│                          │ - Return sorted set.                                                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This should produce a much better ratio than the random baseline.                         │
