│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ nested_interval_sidon                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The prior programs try to build Sidon sets (all pairwise sums distinct, all pairwise      │
│                          │ differences distinct) which maximises |A-A| but also maximises |A+A|, giving a poor       │
│                          │ ratio. The key insight is that we don't need a Sidon set — we need a set whose            │
│                          │ *differences* are mostly distinct while its *sums* are heavily collided.                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I draw inspiration from the Bose–Chowla / quadratic constructions used before, but        │
│                          │ instead of using the standard Sidon set I use a *nested interval* construction: place     │
│                          │ many points inside a few tightly clustered blocks, where within-block differences are     │
│                          │ distinct (giving a large difference set) but sums between blocks collapse onto a small    │
│                          │ number of values because the block offsets are chosen so that sums coincide.              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Concretely, I build A = union over k of (B + k*M) where B is a small Sidon-like base set  │
│                          │ with distinct differences, and M is large enough that differences within one block don't  │
│                          │ collide with differences between blocks. Then |A-A| ≈ K*(|B|-1)*|B| + (K-1)*... which     │
│                          │ grows, while |A+A| stays bounded by roughly (2K-1)*|B+B| + collisions from the            │
│                          │ arithmetic-progression spacing. I additionally run a fast local search that only perturbs │
│                          │ the *block offsets* (not the internal structure), which keeps the difference set large    │
│                          │ while allowing the sum set to shrink via collisions.                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This is fundamentally different from the prior greedy-Sidon / quadratic-Sidon approach    │
│                          │ because it deliberately sacrifices sum-distinctness in favour of sum-collisions, which is │
│                          │ exactly what the ratio metric rewards.                                                    │
