╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ add_bounded_sidon_and_better_local_search                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current program only uses `_quadratic_set` (a Bose-Chowla-like Sidon construction)    │
│                          │ and `_greedy_sidon` with a very loose span (4*n^2), which makes |A+A| large. The prior    │
│                          │ "improved" version had a `_bounded_sidon` that constrains elements to a span near n^2,    │
│                          │ keeping the sum set compact while preserving Sidon-like properties (which maximize        │
│                          │ differences). It also had a `_reflect_half` heuristic and a multi-scale local search.     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I will:                                                                                   │
│                          │ 1. Add a `_bounded_sidon` function that greedily picks elements biased toward the center  │
│                          │ of a tight span, so that |A+A| stays small while |A-A| remains near maximal for a Sidon   │
│                          │ set.                                                                                      │
│                          │ 2. Add a `_reflect_half` function that reflects the upper half of A around its center,    │
│                          │ which can compress the sum set.                                                           │
│                          │ 3. Extend `solve()` to try bounded Sidon constructions over a range of sizes and spans,   │
│                          │ and use a multi-scale local search (alternating small and large step sizes) with          │
│                          │ occasional simulated-annealing-like acceptance, which explores the space more             │
│                          │ effectively.                                                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The theoretical intuition: For a Sidon set A of size n, |A-A| = n^2 - n + 1 (maximal      │
│                          │ possible) and |A+A| = n(n+1)/2. If we additionally confine A to a tight interval, sums    │
│                          │ can collapse further (many coincident sums), boosting the ratio log|A-A|/log|A+A|.        │
│                          │ Bounded Sidon constructions from the prior version achieved 1.1373, better than what the  │
│                          │ current code likely produces.                                                             │
