│ patch_name               │ sidon_reflect_crossover                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The two implementations are nearly identical: the current program has `_reflect_half`     │
│                          │ only, while the inspiration program has the more general `_reflect_fraction` and          │
│                          │ `_multi_reflect` helpers that try multiple reflection fractions and center modes,         │
│                          │ iterated over several rounds. The inspiration version also uses `_multi_reflect` on both  │
│                          │ the quadratic constructions and the final polishing stage.                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The crossover keeps the current program's structure but replaces the single               │
│                          │ `_reflect_half` with the more powerful `_reflect_fraction` + `_multi_reflect` pair from   │
│                          │ the inspiration. This expands the reflection search space (fractions 0.3/0.4/0.5/0.6 and  │
│                          │ four center modes, iterated) which is a cheap structural move that preserves the          │
│                          │ difference multiset (up to sign) while potentially shrinking the sum set — exactly the    │
│                          │ trade-off this problem rewards. The final polishing step also uses `_multi_reflect` with  │
│                          │ 6 rounds instead of a single reflection.                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ To further improve the score, I add a light local-search phase that operates on the       │
│                          │ *multiset of distinct differences* indirectly by trying small perturbations on the best   │
│                          │ set found, and I keep the multi-scale perturbation scheme. I also try a wider grid of     │
│                          │ bounded Sidon spans and seeds, since the bounded Sidon construction is the most direct    │
│                          │ way to keep |A+A| small.                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The result maintains the same `solve()` signature and returns a sorted list of distinct   │
│                          │ integers.                                                                                 │
