├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ relaxed_sidon_diff_collisions                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The current program enforces strict Sidon-ness in `_bounded_sidon`, which forces all      │
│                          │ pairwise differences to be unique. This maximizes |A−A| but also inflates |A+A| because   │
│                          │ Sidon sets spread out over a wide span. The objective log|A−A|/log|A+A| rewards trading a │
│                          │ small loss in |A−A| for a larger relative reduction in |A+A|.                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Idea: relax the difference-uniqueness constraint in `_bounded_sidon` to allow a           │
│                          │ controlled fraction of duplicate differences (track a diff-multiplicity counter and       │
│                          │ permit up to ~20% collisions), while still rejecting any sum collision. This yields a     │
│                          │ denser set inside a tighter span, shrinking |A+A| while keeping |A−A| close to maximal. I │
│                          │ add a `diff_budget` parameter proportional to n and a `diff_counts` dictionary to enforce │
│                          │ it. The rest of the pipeline (quadratic sets, greedy Sidon, reflection, local search) is  │
│                          │ unchanged and still available; the new construction simply provides additional candidate  │
│                          │ sets that the existing scoring/local-search machinery can exploit. I also add a           │
│                          │ `sum_span` penalty bias so candidates near the center are preferred (already partially    │
│                          │ present), and ensure spans are tuned tighter (span_mult 2 and 3 dominate for the relaxed  │
│                          │ variant).                                                                                 │
