│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ multi_cardinality_search                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The current program fixes cardinality implicitly through the structured construction (m + │
│                          │ gs) and lets SA drift the size. The size bonus `(1 - 1/n)/100` is tiny (~0.01 at n=100)   │
│                          │ yet the SA is biased toward whatever size the seed happens to have. Meanwhile, the pure   │
│                          │ log-ratio `log|A-A|/log|A+A|` can behave quite differently at different cardinalities:    │
│                          │ for dense intervals |A-A| ~ 2n and |A+A| ~ 2n giving ratio ~1, but for sparse Golomb-like │
│                          │ sets |A-A| ~ n(n-1) while |A+A| stays much smaller, so larger n can help if we keep sums  │
│                          │ sub-quadratic.                                                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I propose running the local search across a *range* of target cardinalities n ∈ {40, 60,  │
│                          │ 90, 130, 180, 250, 350, 500}, allocating time proportionally to the pure log-ratio (i.e.  │
│                          │ dropping the size bonus from the search objective so it doesn't distort the comparison).  │
│                          │ Allocate a fixed slice of the deadline to each n, always starting from a structured seed  │
│                          │ of exactly that size (dense core of size ~sqrt(n) plus Golomb tail of size n - core), and │
│                          │ keep the best result across all n. This makes the search cardinality-aware and lets us    │
│                          │ discover whether a larger n actually improves the pure ratio, rather than letting SA      │
│                          │ wander in cardinality space.                                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Concretely:                                                                               │
│                          │ 1. Add a `_score_pure(a)` function that returns just `log|A-A|/log|A+A|` (no size bonus). │
│                          │ 2. Add a `_build_of_size(n, rng)` helper that constructs a structured seed with exactly n │
│                          │ elements: core = range(m+1) where m ≈ round(sqrt(n)), tail = Golomb of size n - m - 1     │
│                          │ offset by m + gap, with gap and stride/scale chosen randomly.                             │
│                          │ 3. Add a `_local_search_pure` variant that uses `_score_pure` (so the trajectory is not   │
│                          │ biased by the size bonus) and collects the best set found.                                │
│                          │ 4. In `solve()`, loop over the target cardinalities, giving each one a time slice, and    │
│                          │ keep the overall best by `_score_pure`. Final returned set is the pure-ratio winner (the  │
│                          │ actual grading still adds the size bonus, which we accept as a small bonus on top).       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This should let the search explore the cardinality axis directly, which the current       │
│                          │ program does only accidentally.                                                           │
