│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ reflect_aggressive_sa                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The key insight from the prior work is that `_reflect_half` (reflecting a subset of A     │
│                          │ about a center to alter the sum set while roughly preserving the difference set) is a     │
│                          │ cheap, difference-preserving structural move. In the current program it's barely used. I  │
│                          │ generalize it into a full reflection search:                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. For any set A and any center c, the operation A -> (A \ S) ∪ {2c - x : x ∈ S} for a    │
│                          │ subset S preserves the *multiset of differences within S* and within A\S, but changes     │
│                          │ cross-differences. Crucially, when c is chosen as the midpoint of a "dense cluster"       │
│                          │ inside A, the reflected cluster's sums with other elements often collide with existing    │
│                          │ sums, shrinking |A+A| while |A-A| stays large.                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. I enumerate many reflection centers (midpoints of pairs, medians of prefixes,          │
│                          │ quantiles of the sorted set) and reflection fractions (top 20%, 30%, 40%, 50% of the      │
│                          │ sorted set, plus bottom fractions), evaluate each candidate with the fast scorer, and     │
│                          │ greedily apply the best improvement. This is iterated as a hill-climb that repeatedly     │
│                          │ shaves |A+A|.                                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. I keep the strong structured seed family (dense core + Golomb tail with stride/scale   │
│                          │ variants) from the prior version, plus the cardinality-aware SA using the pure ratio, and │
│                          │ interleave reflection hill-climbing with SA restarts.                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 4. Reflection is applied both to the structured seeds (a cheap way to get a much better   │
│                          │ starting point) and as a final polish on the SA best.                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The result is a program that exploits the reflection move far more aggressively while     │
│                          │ retaining the proven dense+Golomb base and SA.                                            │
