│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ targeted_sum_compaction                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The current program relies on random element perturbations in its final local search,     │
│                          │ which only weakly attacks the denominator |A+A| of the log-ratio. Prior feedback flags    │
│                          │ this as the binding constraint. I propose adding a targeted sum-set compaction move to    │
│                          │ the local-search loop: pick a random index i, compute the current difference set and sum  │
│                          │ set, then scan candidate replacement values v in a small window around a and pick the v   │
│                          │ that minimizes |A+A| subject to keeping |A-A| at least 95% of its current size. This      │
│                          │ directly reduces the denominator while protecting the numerator, and is cheap since we    │
│                          │ maintain base_diffs/base_sums incrementally over the candidate window. I also add a       │
│                          │ periodic "compaction sweep" that iterates over all indices once with the same rule.       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ To implement this efficiently I compute the multiset contribution of each element to      │
│                          │ sums/diffs. For a candidate v replacing a, the new sum set is (base_sums minus sums       │
│                          │ involving a) union {v + x for x in A\{a}}. Since A is small (typically <= few hundred), a │
│                          │ full recompute per candidate is acceptable within the 120s budget if we cap the window.   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I add a helper `_sum_compaction_step(A, rng, window=30)` that performs one such move, and │
│                          │ call it inside the final local-search loop with some probability, interleaved with the    │
│                          │ existing random perturbation. This keeps the existing exploration while adding a greedy   │
│                          │ descent on |A+A|.                                                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The window size and the 0.95 floor are tunable; I set window=25 and floor=0.97 to be      │
│                          │ conservative so we do not degrade |A-A| too much. I also add a small trial count so the   │
│                          │ cost stays bounded.                                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The helper must be defined before `solve()` uses it. I add it just before `solve()`.      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Additionally, I replace the existing final local-search block with a version that         │
│                          │ alternates random perturbation and `_sum_compaction_step`. The rest of the program is     │
│                          │ unchanged.                                                                                │
