                        Patch Metadata - Gen 34/1000 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/1                        
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ additive_combinatorics_hybrid                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ This approach abandons the greedy/local-search paradigm entirely and instead exploits a   │
│                          │ fundamental additive-combinatorics identity: for a Sidon set (all pairwise sums           │
│                          │ distinct), |A+A| = n(n+1)/2 and |A-A| = n(n-1)+1, giving ratio ~2. This is the            │
│                          │ theoretical maximum for the sum/difference ratio. So the optimal strategy is to build the │
│                          │ *largest* Sidon set possible within a bounded integer range, then apply small             │
│                          │ "sum-compacting" perturbations that preserve the Sidon property locally but reduce |A+A|  │
│                          │ slightly (e.g., by introducing a few coincidental sums while keeping all differences      │
│                          │ distinct).                                                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The key insight: Bose-Chowla / Singer constructions give Sidon sets of size p in range    │
│                          │ ~p². We use the Ruzsa construction A = {2pk + (k² mod p) : k=0..p-1} which is a perfect   │
│                          │ difference set (|A-A| = p(p-1)+1) with |A+A| ≤ p(p+1)/2 + extra. We sweep p up to ~1000   │
│                          │ (yielding sets of size ~1000 with |A-A| ~ 10^6 and |A+A| ~ 5×10^5), then apply a final    │
│                          │ "sum compaction" pass that tries to shrink |A+A| without losing differences by replacing  │
│                          │ one element with a nearby value that shares many sums.                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This is fundamentally different: instead of stochastic search it uses a deterministic     │
│                          │ algebraic construction with provably near-optimal sum/difference ratio, scaled to         │
│                          │ maximize the log-ratio (which grows with size due to the (1-1/|A|)/100 bonus).            │
